{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And they sat to eat bread.</b> None of this was in their eyes a stumbling block or obstacle that would prevent them from fixing their meal — as would have been fitting for the righteous when a mishap occurs through them, as Israel did after the killing of the tribe of Benjamin, as it says: וַיֵּשְׁבוּ עַד הָעֶרֶב לִפְנֵי הָאֱלֹהִים וַיִּשְׂאוּ קוֹלָם וַיִּבְכּוּ בְּכִי גָדוֹל וַיֹּאמְרוּ לָמָה ה' אֱלֹהֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל הָיְתָה זֹאת בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל (Judges 21:2–3). So too Darius when he cast Daniel into the lions' den, as it is written: וּבָת טְוָת וְדַחֲוָן לָא הַנְעֵל קָדָמוֹהִי (Daniel 6:19). And this happened to them because they considered Joseph a pursuer whom all who strike first to kill are justified — when there is no way to save the pursued other than this." "<b>Going down to Egypt.</b> They were owners of the camels and not themselves merchants; therefore the bringing down of merchandise there was the completion of their task."], :book :genesis, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {0 "Sforno quotes Daniel 6:19 in the Aramaic of the biblical text; translated here literally as he cites it. The phrase describes Darius fasting through the night."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:33:43.378563506Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}